%I A400127 #44 Oct 22 2026 21:40:44
%S A400127 2,2,5,9,14,25,39,50,62,101,170,222,379,498,679,1003,1419,2162,2888,
%T A400127 4115,6247,9180,14837,18281,27899,39821,55361,101619,117606
%N A400127 Least m such that every binary word of length n occurs as a contiguous subword of the binary representation of 3^k for some 0 <= k <= m.
%C A400127 For n = 3,...,18, exactly 2^n - 1 words have occurred by m = a(n)-1, and the single remaining word first occurs in 3^a(n). For n = 2, three words first occur at m = 2.
%C A400127 Conjecture: a(n) is finite for every n.
%C A400127 This conjecture follows from the density of the fractional parts of k*log_2(3). Choose a binary de Bruijn sequence of order n, linearized and rotated to begin with 1. Since log_2(3) is irrational, infinitely many powers 3^k have this finite binary string as their initial digits. Such a power alone therefore contains every n-bit binary word, proving a(n) < infinity for every n. - _David Rabahy_, Oct 04 2026
%H A400127 Ralf Stephan, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14774">Aperiodicity and subword complexity in the binary expansion of powers of three</a>, arXiv:2607.14774 [math.CO], 2026.
%e A400127 a(3) = 5. By 3^4, seven of the eight binary words of length 3 have occurred. The missing word 111 occurs in 3^5 = 243 = 11110011 (base 2), so a(3) = 5.
%e A400127 a(10) = 101. The final binary word of length 10 to appear is 0000000000.
%o A400127 (Python)
%o A400127 def a(n):
%o A400127     seen = set()
%o A400127     x = 1
%o A400127     m = 0
%o A400127     target_count = 2**n
%o A400127     while True:
%o A400127         s = bin(x)[2:]
%o A400127         # Update set with all n-length substrings
%o A400127         seen.update(s[i:i+n] for i in range(len(s) - n + 1))
%o A400127         if len(seen) == target_count:
%o A400127             return m
%o A400127         x *= 3
%o A400127         m += 1
%o A400127 for n in range(1, 19):
%o A400127     print(f"n={n}: m={a(n)}")
%Y A400127 Cf. A004656 (powers of 3 written in binary), A156301 (first appearance of an n-bit subword), A052944 (for minimum-length binary de Bruijn strings), A058891 (the number of leading-1 linearizations of binary de Bruijn cycles of order n).
%K A400127 nonn,base,more,changed
%O A400127 1,1
